.TH THRSSTY 1 "March 2026" "thrssty 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
thrssty \- TUI RSS/Atom newsreader for the terminal
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B thrssty
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B thrssty
is a terminal-based RSS/Atom newsreader with a three-panel layout inspired by the
Mutt email client.
The viewport is divided into a
.B feed sidebar
(left),
an
.B article list
(top right), and an
.B article view
(bottom right).
.PP
Navigating the feed sidebar updates the article list automatically.
Moving the cursor in the article list renders the selected article immediately.
Switching to a new feed auto-selects its first unread article.
.PP
All key bindings, colours, and panel proportions are configurable via
.IR config.toml .
Helix-editor theme files are supported natively.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version string and exit.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-refresh
Refresh all configured feeds immediately on startup, overriding the
.I refresh_on_start
setting in the config file.
.TP
.BI \-c " path" "\fR,\fP \-\-config " path
Load the specified file instead of the default config.
Config values cascade: built-in defaults < current-directory
.I config.toml
< user config
.RI ( ~/.config/thrssty/config.toml )
< this file.
.TP
.BI \-t " path\fR|\fPname" "\fR,\fP \-\-theme " path\fR|\fPname
Override the theme.
Accepts a bare theme name (looked up in
.IR ~/.config/thrssty/themes/ )
or an absolute/relative path to a
.I .toml
file.
Helix themes (e.g.\&
.IR /usr/lib/helix/runtime/themes/ayu_evolve.toml )
are resolved directly, including
.I inherits
chains.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-init
Write default
.IR config.toml ,
.IR themes/default.toml " (built-in theme),"
and an example
.I feeds.toml
to
.IR ~/.config/thrssty/ ,
then exit.
Existing files are not overwritten.
.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
All bindings shown are the compiled-in defaults; every key is configurable
in the
.B [keys]
section of
.IR config.toml .
.SS Feed sidebar
.TP
.B j
Move cursor down in the feed list.
.TP
.B k
Move cursor up in the feed list.
.SS Article list
.TP
.B \(->  (Right arrow)
Move cursor down in the article list.
.TP
.B \(<-  (Left arrow)
Move cursor up in the article list.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
Jump to the first / last article.
.SS Article view
.TP
.B \(da  (Down arrow)
Scroll article view down one line.
.TP
.B \(ua  (Up arrow)
Scroll article view up one line.
.TP
.BR PageDown ", " PageUp
Scroll article view down / up one page.
.SS Global
.TP
.B r
Refresh the currently selected feed.
.TP
.B R
Refresh all feeds.
.TP
.B a
Add a new feed.
.B thrssty
prompts for a URL in the status bar, validates it in the background, then
optionally prompts for a category name.
The feed is written to
.I feeds.toml
immediately; the file is created if it does not yet exist.
.TP
.B d
Delete the currently selected feed.
A confirmation prompt is shown before the feed is removed from
.IR feeds.toml .
.TP
.B e
Edit the display name of the selected feed. The current name is pre-filled
in the input line; press Enter to confirm or Esc to cancel.
An empty input resets the name to the feed's own title on the next refresh.
.PP
.B Input line editing
.PP
All text prompts
.RB ( a ,
.BR e ,
category name)
support standard readline\-style editing: arrow keys move the cursor,
.B Ctrl+A
/
.B Ctrl+E
jump to start/end,
.B Ctrl+W
deletes the previous word,
.B Ctrl+U
clears the line, and
.B Ctrl+K
kills to end of line.
.TP
.B o
Open the selected article URL in the system browser
.RB ( $BROWSER
or
.BR xdg-open ).
.TP
.B F5
Reload the configuration and feeds files without restarting.
Useful after editing
.I config.toml
or
.I feeds.toml
by hand.
.TP
.B q
Quit
.BR thrssty .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/thrssty/config.toml
Primary user configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/thrssty/feeds.toml
Feed list.
Optional — the app starts with an empty sidebar if the file is absent.
Use
.B a
in the UI to add feeds interactively; they are written to this file automatically.
.TP
.I ~/.config/thrssty/themes/
Directory for user-supplied theme files.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/thrssty/cache.json
Article and read-state cache, written on clean exit and after each
successful feed refresh.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B thrssty
is configured with a TOML file.
Use
.B thrssty \-\-init
to generate a commented example at
.IR ~/.config/thrssty/config.toml .
.PP
Key bindings in the
.B [keys]
section accept key names such as
.IR q ,
.IR j ,
.IR Up ,
.IR Down ,
.IR Left ,
.IR Right ,
.IR Home ,
.IR End ,
.IR PageUp ,
.IR PageDown ,
and modifier combinations such as
.IR ctrl-r .
.
.SH THEMING
Themes are TOML files that define a
.B [palette]
and a set of UI scope entries.
.B thrssty
understands a subset of the
.UR https://docs.helix-editor.com/master/themes.html
Helix editor theme format
.UE ,
including the
.I inherits
key for theme inheritance chains.
.PP
The
.B theme
config key accepts:
.TP
.B auto
Detect the terminal background color and select the matching built-in theme
.RB ( default_dark
on dark terminals,
.B default_light
on light terminals).
This is the default.
.TP
.B default_dark
Always use the built-in dark theme (Ao-inspired dark palette).
.TP
.B default_light
Always use the built-in light theme.
.TP
.BI name
Look up
.I ~/.config/thrssty/themes/<name>.toml
.RB ( purple
ships as an example in the repository).
.TP
.I /path/to/theme.toml
Use the theme at the given path directly.
.PP
Helix system themes can be used directly:
.PP
.RS
.EX
theme = "/usr/lib/helix/runtime/themes/ayu_evolve.toml"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Unmapped Helix scopes are resolved automatically to their closest
.B thrssty
equivalent.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BROWSER
Preferred browser, consulted before falling back to
.BR xdg-open .
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate default config files and launch:
.PP
.RS
.EX
thrssty --init
$EDITOR ~/.config/thrssty/feeds.toml
thrssty
.EE
.RE
.PP
Launch with an alternative config and refresh all feeds immediately:
.PP
.RS
.EX
thrssty --config ~/work/thrssty.toml --refresh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Try a Helix theme without changing the config:
.PP
.RS
.EX
thrssty --theme /usr/lib/helix/runtime/themes/catppuccin_mocha.toml
.EE
.RE
.
